  "status": "independently reviewed; admitted CUDA local-response attribution",
  "declared_at": "2026-11-16T17:19:23.418903-06:00",
  "why_this_direction": "Native replay reproduced both retained13-column branches exactly, locating an early CPU/native norm discrepancy but not a learner defect. The actual learner is CUDA-based, so compare its unchanged finite response and local JVP first, then test whether only the native norm operand corrects its response.",
  "repeating_pattern_check": "Higher cosine and near-equal primals repeatedly masked large directional errors, and CPU evidence alone cannot justify a CUDA learner change. Retain original local JVP, complete selected-call weighted residual and every stage; keep the norm substitution finite-only and do not mistake native primals for derivatives.",
  "hypothesis": "If normalization primals are a sufficient cause of the live CUDA adapter response error, replacing only the complete normalized operand by the retained native value at both endpoints improves gate/up and downstream native-response agreement. If the CUDA baseline already agrees, the CPU-specific difference is not a justified learner correction.",
  "what_went_wrong_before": "CPU13-column finite weighted response opposed native, but the GPU1 native graph matches both savedGPU0 branches bit-for-bit. Both original and replay finite/JVP comparisons contain non-smooth precision effects; exact merged primals have not qualified their derivatives.",
  "rule": "Use exact retained13-event189/190 inputs and actual local BF16 displacement. Run only baseline and native_norm cases with the active CUDA adapter. Baseline includes original observed-input local JVP; native_norm is a finite intervention only. Preserve global reference/multiplicity/105-merge mass for the local contribution. GPU1 blocking lock; no native acquisition, weight update, rate choice or active-source change.",
  "prediction": "The measured actual CUDA baseline and one finite norm substitution distinguish a learner normalization-operand mismatch from a CPU-only replay artifact or another local operator effect. Results are reported without an automatic fidelity threshold.",
  "would_weaken_it": "CUDA baseline agreement would weaken normalization as a learner defect; no downstream improvement from native_norm would weaken normalization as a sufficient local cause. Finite improvement without JVP/native computational agreement does not qualify a gradient fix or fresh learning improvement.",
